WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.934
A private RAG system has many moving parts: source connectors, ingestion jobs, parsers, chunkers, embedding generation, vector stores, metadata stores, retrieval services, model endpoints, user interfaces, logs, evaluation jobs, and administration workflows.

2
00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:21.568
Each component needs ownership and failure behavior.

3
00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:25.360
Operations starts with refresh and lifecycle planning.

4
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.969
Sources change, documents expire, permissions move, and old guidance is replaced.

5
00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:41.556
The system should define refresh schedules, incremental indexing, deletion workflows, stale-content handling, and review for sources that fail to update.

6
00:00:41.556 --> 00:00:46.691
A RAG system that cannot remove or update knowledge reliably will lose trust.

7
00:00:46.691 --> 00:00:59.726
Reliability planning should cover source outages, parser failures, embedding failures, index corruption, model unavailability, slow retrieval, storage limits, and failed background jobs.

8
00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:04.941
Users may not see these components directly, but their answers depend on them.

9
00:01:04.941 --> 00:01:12.051
Monitoring and alerts should make important failures visible before users quietly receive poor results.

10
00:01:12.051 --> 00:01:14.974
Versioning helps teams understand change.

11
00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:22.163
Models, prompts, indexes, chunking rules, retrieval settings, and evaluation sets may all affect output.

12
00:01:22.163 --> 00:01:28.878
Recording versions makes it easier to compare behavior, explain changes, and roll back when needed.

13
00:01:28.878 --> 00:01:31.723
Reliability is also a governance concern.

14
00:01:31.723 --> 00:01:43.020
Users may rely on retrieved answers for real work, so backup, restore, rollback, cost controls, incident response, and communication plans belong in the architecture.

15
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.392
Private RAG becomes dependable when operations are planned, owned, and tested.

16
00:01:48.392 --> 00:01:56.372
Operational runbooks should name owners, normal recovery steps, escalation paths, and communication expectations.

17
00:01:56.372 --> 00:02:07.116
When retrieval quality drops or a connector fails, the team should know whether to pause the workflow, serve degraded results, or direct users to source systems.
